## Session proxies

`Session.fromPartition(partition).setProxy()` sends every request from views
in that partition through a proxy: page loads, subresources, `fetch()` and
WebSockets. The proxy also resolves the hostnames, so an app can run a local
proxy that maps names such as `feature-a.localhost` to different local ports
without touching the system's DNS or proxy settings.

```ts
import { BrowserView, Session } from "electrobun/main";

if (Session.supportsProxy) {
  Session.fromPartition("persist:preview").setProxy({
    type: "http",
    host: "127.0.0.1",
    port: 8899,
    matchDomains: ["localhost"],
  });
}

const view = new BrowserView({
  url: "http://feature-a.localhost/",
  partition: "persist:preview",
});
```

| Field | Type | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `type` | `"http" \| "socks5"` | `http` opens every connection, plain HTTP included, with `CONNECT`. |
| `host`, `port` | `string`, `number` | The proxy's address. |
| `matchDomains` | `string[]` | Only these domains and their subdomains use the proxy. |
| `excludedDomains` | `string[]` | These domains and their subdomains never use the proxy. |

The proxy applies to views already using the partition and to views created
later. Pass `null` to connect directly again. The setting lasts until the app
quits.

App Transport Security still applies to the URL. On macOS, a bundled app can
load plain `http` only from `localhost` and IP addresses unless its Info.plist
has an exception, so names like `feature-a.localhost` need an
`NSExceptionDomains` entry, which a `postBuild` hook can add.

Session proxies need macOS 14 or later and apply to the native WebKit
renderer. `Session.supportsProxy` is `false` on older macOS, Linux and
Windows, and `setProxy()` throws there rather than loading pages without the
proxy. CEF views don't use the session proxy.

## Partition storage

`Session.fromPartition(partition)` manages the storage behind a `partition`.
`clearStorageData()` waits for the native engine to finish removing data and
returns the outcome. Treat anything other than `"cleared"` as data that may
still be there:

```ts
import { Session } from "electrobun/main";

const result = Session.fromPartition("persist:account-a").clearStorageData();
if (result.status !== "cleared") {
  console.warn("Storage may remain", result);
}
```

| Status | Reason | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cleared` | | The engine reported that removal finished. |
| `failed` | `timeout` | No completion within 10 seconds. Removal may still finish later. |
| `failed` | `invalid-types` | The list was empty or named a type the platform can't clear. Nothing was removed. |
| `unsupported` | `partition` | Only `persist:` partitions can be cleared after creation. |
| `unsupported` | `platform` | Linux and Windows don't report completion yet, so the data may remain. |

On macOS 14 and later, each `persist:` partition has its own WebKit data
store, named by a UUID derived from the partition name. To choose the store
yourself, for example to keep test runs away from a user's data, call
`setStorageIdentifier()` before creating the first webview for the partition.
Read `getStorageIdentifier()` on a view to confirm which store it really uses:

```ts
import { BrowserView, Session } from "electrobun/main";

const session = Session.fromPartition("persist:account-a");
const assigned = session.setStorageIdentifier(crypto.randomUUID());

const view = new BrowserView({ partition: "persist:account-a" });
if (assigned.status === "set") {
  console.log(view.getStorageIdentifier() === assigned.identifier);
}
```

`setStorageIdentifier()` returns the identifier in the uppercase form
`getStorageIdentifier()` reports. It returns `failed` with `in-use` once any
native webview has used the partition with a different store in this process,
and `unsupported` on macOS 13 and older, on Linux, on Windows, and for
partitions without the `persist:` prefix. CEF views ignore the identifier.
On macOS 13 and older every `persist:` partition shares the default store.
`getStorageIdentifier()` returns `null` wherever no identifier applies,
including CEF views. Both are TypeScript-only.

## Isolated preview views

An isolated preview shows a document the app doesn't trust, such as
generated HTML, with its pages and assets served by the app instead of a
server. The view is throwaway and sandboxed. Native code blocks every way the
page could reach out before it loads, so page script and response headers
can't lift the blocks:

- Documents, subresources, `fetch`, workers and iframes load only from the
  preview's own scheme, plus `about:blank`, `about:srcdoc`, `data:` and
  `blob:`. Every other load, including WebSockets, beacons and preconnects, is
  blocked by a WebKit content rule list that is installed before the first
  navigation.
- WebRTC, WebAssembly, WebGL and service workers are unavailable, because the
  page runs in WebKit Lockdown Mode.
- Storage is a fresh non-persistent data store for each view, shared with no
  other view and discarded with it. The `partition` option is ignored.
- Top-level and frame navigations off the scheme are cancelled, as are
  cmd-clicks, popups and downloads. None of them produce a `new-window-open` or
  download event.
- Camera, microphone, location and file-picker requests are denied without a
  prompt.
- `views://` and `appdata://` are not available, and RPC is disabled as with
  `sandbox: true`.

Each request to the scheme is passed to the handler as a standard `Request`,
and its `Response` is returned to the page as is. Requests that arrive while
the view has no handler get a 404. Request bodies up to 16 MB are forwarded
when WebKit hands them to the scheme handler, which it does for string bodies
but not for every body type; larger bodies get a 413. The scheme must be lowercase letters,
digits and hyphens, start with a letter, and not be a scheme WebKit handles
itself (such as `https` or `file`), `views` or `appdata`.

```ts
import { BrowserView } from "electrobun/main";
import { file } from "bun";

if (BrowserView.isIsolatedPreviewSupported()) {
  const preview = new BrowserView({
    windowId: win.id,
    url: "app-preview://report/index.html",
    isolatedPreview: {
      scheme: "app-preview",
      handler: async (request) => {
        const path = new URL(request.url).pathname;
        const asset = file(`/path/to/report${path}`);
        return (await asset.exists())
          ? new Response(asset)
          : new Response("Not found", { status: 404 });
      },
    },
  });
  void preview;
}
```

The handler decides which files the preview can read, so check that every
resolved path stays inside the folder you mean to share. To answer requests for
a preview created by an
[`<electrobun-webview isolated-preview>`](/electrobun/apis/browser/electrobun-webview-tag/#isolated-previews)
tag, call `view.setIsolatedPreviewHandler(handler)` from a `BrowserView.on("created")`
listener. A preview refuses `loadHTML()`.

Isolated previews are supported only by the native renderer on macOS.
`BrowserView.isIsolatedPreviewSupported()` returns `false` on Linux and
Windows, and creating one there, or with `renderer: "cef"`, throws.
