## User agent

A view can send its own User-Agent instead of the engine's. Set it at creation
with the `userAgent` option, so even the first request carries it, or change it
later with `setUserAgent()`. A change applies from the view's next request on;
reload or navigate to send it for the current page. Pass `null` to go back to
the engine default. The same string is what pages read from
`navigator.userAgent`.

`BrowserView.getDefaultUserAgent()` returns the user agent the native renderer
uses when no custom one is set, so an app can derive its own from it.

```ts
import { BrowserView } from "electrobun/main";

const engineUserAgent = BrowserView.getDefaultUserAgent();

const view = new BrowserView({
  url: "https://example.com",
  userAgent: engineUserAgent ? `${engineUserAgent} MyApp/1.0` : null,
});

if (!view.setUserAgent(null)) {
  // This renderer/platform can't change the user agent.
}
```

Only the macOS native renderer (WKWebView) supports custom user agents. On
CEF, Linux and Windows, `setUserAgent()` returns `false` and changes nothing,
the `userAgent` option logs a warning and keeps the engine default, and
`getDefaultUserAgent()` returns `null`. `view.userAgent` always holds the
custom user agent in effect, or `null`.
